WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.089 --> 00:00:02.979
Film Courage: Larry why do you feel that BEETLEJUICE
sold?

00:00:02.979 --> 00:00:08.930
Why did the screenplay sell (in your opinion)?

00:00:08.930 --> 00:00:13.250
Larry Wilson: You know…this is going to
sound so pretentious.

00:00:13.250 --> 00:00:22.380
But when I decided I was going to be a writer
again and decided I was going to do it with

00:00:22.380 --> 00:00:23.380
Michael [McDowell].

00:00:23.380 --> 00:00:28.500
In my mind I said to myself…(this sounds
so awful maybe) but “I’m going to write

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:29.500
a classic.

00:00:29.500 --> 00:00:31.570
I’m not just going to write another movie.

00:00:31.570 --> 00:00:34.510
We are going to going to create a classic.”

00:00:34.510 --> 00:00:35.750
And who knows what that means?

00:00:35.750 --> 00:00:38.870
I know it sounds obnoxious.

00:00:38.870 --> 00:00:54.320
But we knew probably…I don’t know…some
20 pages in that just something was happening.

00:00:54.320 --> 00:00:56.680
And we weren’t thinking about demographics.

00:00:56.680 --> 00:01:00.059
We weren’t thinking about who the audience
was going to be.

00:01:00.059 --> 00:01:07.820
We weren’t thinking about any “commercial”
aspects of it.

00:01:07.820 --> 00:01:09.590
We were thinking What’s good?

00:01:09.590 --> 00:01:10.799
What’s funny?

00:01:10.799 --> 00:01:14.249
What’s moving the story forward?

00:01:14.249 --> 00:01:18.229
What are the zaniest, craziest ideas we could
have.

00:01:18.229 --> 00:01:25.770
And it happens with scripts and it happens
in your writing.

00:01:25.770 --> 00:01:33.789
There are just times that you feel that you’re
in a zone and place that you are so connected

00:01:33.789 --> 00:01:34.789
with yourself.

00:01:34.789 --> 00:01:41.170
And you are so connect with what your vision
is, that if you can get it on a page, other

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:46.509
people are going to see it and feel it to.

00:01:46.509 --> 00:01:50.270
And that movie as that.

00:01:50.270 --> 00:01:53.340
We didn’t know what was going to happen
with it.

00:01:53.340 --> 00:01:57.090
When the movie was finished there were people
at Warner Brothers in the marketing department

00:01:57.090 --> 00:01:59.139
that absolutely hated it.

00:01:59.139 --> 00:02:03.590
They wanted to change the title the HOUSE
GHOST and dump it.

00:02:03.590 --> 00:02:07.919
And back in the days when you’d put it in
a thousand theaters and let it run a week

00:02:07.919 --> 00:02:10.929
and hopefully it would go away.

00:02:10.929 --> 00:02:16.670
That wasn’t everyone and we had David Geffen
on our side so we were protected but it was

00:02:16.670 --> 00:02:31.079
hardly loved but I knew who we were writing
it for in my mind.

00:02:31.079 --> 00:02:38.770
This is a very 80’s reference…there was
a band (there is still a band) The Cure, The

00:02:38.770 --> 00:02:43.349
(great) Cure (a band I loved).

00:02:43.349 --> 00:02:47.090
And I had gone to see them at the [Pasadena]
Rosebowl.

00:02:47.090 --> 00:02:50.700
A huge concert (maybe 50,000 people).

00:02:50.700 --> 00:02:54.060
And it felt to me that it was 50,000 teenage
girls in black!

00:02:54.060 --> 00:02:59.370
Film Courage: I was probably one of them!

00:02:59.370 --> 00:03:00.819
I was into The Cure [Laughs].

00:03:00.819 --> 00:03:01.819
Larry Wilson: Right!

00:03:01.819 --> 00:03:05.390
And the Winona Ryder character “Lydia”
came out of that.

00:03:05.390 --> 00:03:14.810
And I always thought that’s who the audience
for this movie is as we were writing it.

00:03:14.810 --> 00:03:20.890
That for me (and maybe for Michael) that was
who I thought this movie was being written

00:03:20.890 --> 00:03:22.250
for.

00:03:22.250 --> 00:03:27.049
And it’s really a family story and it’s
really about a girl who needs a mom and a

00:03:27.049 --> 00:03:35.950
dad because her real mom and dad don’t understand
her and don’t spend enough time with her

00:03:35.950 --> 00:03:37.609
so she ends up with two ghosts.

00:03:37.609 --> 00:03:44.230
That’s the heart of the movie and that is
what I was writing toward.

00:03:44.230 --> 00:03:51.610
So finally it’s released and I go to see
it, nearby where we are sitting now at Universal.

00:03:51.610 --> 00:03:53.349
It’s a Friday night.

00:03:53.349 --> 00:03:56.569
It’s the the first Friday night.

00:03:56.569 --> 00:03:59.830
And I watch it with the first audience (a
real audience for the first time).

00:03:59.830 --> 00:04:02.400
Of course there had been some test audiences.

00:04:02.400 --> 00:04:06.230
And people are coming out of it either loving
it or hating it which I thought “Great!

00:04:06.230 --> 00:04:11.630
That’s exactly what we wanted.”

00:04:11.630 --> 00:04:14.099
And that was Friday night.

00:04:14.099 --> 00:04:21.459
Then Saturday night I went back and saw a
bunch of teenage girls who had gone into their

00:04:21.459 --> 00:04:28.160
closet (having seen in on Friday night) and
came back dressed like Winona Ryder [the character

00:04:28.160 --> 00:04:29.490
Lydia Deetz].

00:04:29.490 --> 00:04:31.220
And I thought “Okay.

00:04:31.220 --> 00:04:34.520
It’s a hit!

00:04:34.520 --> 00:04:41.470
We have connected with the audience to the
point where there are already dressing up

00:04:41.470 --> 00:04:43.590
as Lydia.”

00:04:43.590 --> 00:04:48.889
And then there was a marketing meeting at
Warner Brothers (it may have been on a Monday).

00:04:48.889 --> 00:04:57.210
And it had surpassed everyone’s expectations
in terms of the grosses and the audience feedback.

00:04:57.210 --> 00:05:03.740
And it was obvious that it was going to take
off and be something special.

00:05:03.740 --> 00:05:14.610
And again in this “no-name zone” [Laughs]…there
was this marketing person who was a guy who

00:05:14.610 --> 00:05:19.970
I remember would wear a scarf no matter what
the temperature was, okay?

00:05:19.970 --> 00:05:24.970
And I remember Tim Burton being there and
David Geffen being there, “The Michaels,’

00:05:24.970 --> 00:05:28.250
and myself.

00:05:28.250 --> 00:05:36.169
And this marketing person, all he could say,
the best he could do was say “Well, at least

00:05:36.169 --> 00:05:39.490
we got the little girls in black to come see.”

00:05:39.490 --> 00:05:48.479
And we walk out of the meeting and Tim said
“Well, who the “F” did he think we made

00:05:48.479 --> 00:05:51.970
it for?”

00:05:51.970 --> 00:05:55.250
[Laughs].

00:05:55.250 --> 00:06:03.580
And when things are going well in my writing
(and I think it’s true for all writers),

00:06:03.580 --> 00:06:10.941
if you are intensely self-conscious about
who is your audience, who is…”is this

00:06:10.941 --> 00:06:12.699
for the 12 to 16-year-olds?”

00:06:12.699 --> 00:06:15.349
If you’re doing that you are not writing
well.

00:06:15.349 --> 00:06:18.301
And you get in a zone and your story starts
telling you.

00:06:18.301 --> 00:06:20.430
You are not telling the story.

00:06:20.430 --> 00:06:25.400
It’s emerging from some place within you.

00:06:25.400 --> 00:06:31.349
And that’s what BEETLEJUICE was and I used
to say to Michael sometimes because I could

00:06:31.349 --> 00:06:35.150
feel it going well and I’d say “Michael,
you know we’re going to have a toy line.”

00:06:35.150 --> 00:06:36.150
And he’d say “Shut up!

00:06:36.150 --> 00:06:37.150
Shut up!

00:06:37.150 --> 00:06:38.909
Let’s just finish the script.”

00:06:38.909 --> 00:06:40.710
You know?

00:06:40.710 --> 00:06:44.720
But it’s a very hard thing to explain.

00:06:44.720 --> 00:06:52.550
It’s an epiphany after epiphany and you
feel like you’re just onto something bigger

00:06:52.550 --> 00:06:53.610
than you.

00:06:53.610 --> 00:06:59.000
Film Courage: Well going back to the other
“no-name person,” did they ever reach

00:06:59.000 --> 00:07:00.000
out to you [after the success of BEETLEJUICE].

00:07:00.000 --> 00:07:05.500
Here they put you in a spot that you’re
almost on a ledge (metaphorically).

00:07:05.500 --> 00:07:13.659
And you’re just elated with validation that
this worked (maybe you didn’t totally expect

00:07:13.659 --> 00:07:14.659
it).

00:07:14.659 --> 00:07:17.009
But did that person ever reach out to you?

00:07:17.009 --> 00:07:22.930
Larry Wilson: No…because that would have
required a certain amount of humility and

00:07:22.930 --> 00:07:28.820
a certain willingness to say “You know what,
I guessed wrong on this one.”

00:07:28.820 --> 00:07:30.919
And not everyone has that ability.

